    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d114">

                <pb facs="0111" n="103" sameAs="frus:frus1941v07/pg103"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">837.20/174</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Chief of the Division of the American
                        Republics</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bonsal</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Ambassador in Cuba</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Messersmith</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-06-16">June 16, 1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Ambassador</hi>: I have your letter of June
                    5, 1941,<anchor n="9" xml:id="d114-f1"/> with which was enclosed a copy of your
                    letter of the same date to Mr. Welles,<anchor n="9" xml:id="d114-f2"/> and of
                    your despatch no. 2132<anchor n="9" xml:id="d114-f3"/> concerning a United
                    States service mission for Cuba and your failure to this date to receive any
                    word from the Navy Department on the supplementary zone around the Guantánamo
                    Naval Station.</p>
                <p>I concur with your suggestion in the last paragraph of despatch no. 2132 to the
                    effect that action on the mission may be deferred until the Cuban Government
                    again takes the initiative.</p>
                <p>In addition to the suggestions relative to the scope of a mission that might be
                    made by the Cuban authorities, suggestions should be expected from your military
                    and naval attachés as to proper functions. The nature of the training required
                    in Cuba in conjunction with the military material to be furnished under the
                    Lend-Lease program should have an important bearing on your Attaché’s
                    suggestions.</p>
                <p>Orme Wilson<anchor n="10" xml:id="d114-f4"/> informs me that Captain Weyler of
                    the Naval Station at Guantánamo is now in Washington. Mr. Wilson has received
                    word from Navy that the matter of the zone is under consideration by Captain
                    Weyler, Captain Schuirmann and Captain McGuire, and that something definite will
                    be ready by June 17. Should this be the case, I will be glad to communicate with
                    you further in this regard.</p>
                <p>With cordial regards,</p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer><signed>P[<hi rendition="#small-caps">hilip</hi>] W B[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">onsal</hi>]</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
